# Symbulator circuit book

title: Circuits for Lesson 10 of the Tutorial

[AS7's Example 13.1 (mutual inductance in jOhm)]

e,1,0,12
r1,1,2,-4j
r2,2,0,5j
m,r2,r3,3j
r3,3,0,6j
r4,3,0,12

analysis: ac
note: In this problem, we describe a mutual inductance (the m element) for the first time. Calculate the phasor currents I1 and I2 — aa(ir2) and aa(ir4). The coils are given in jOhm, so they are r elements with imaginary values and the mutual matches. The dotted end of each coupled element is named first.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/as7e1301.png
omega: omega
rounding: 4
si: no
units: yes
rms: no
polar: yes


[AS7's Practice Problem 13.1]

e,1,0,(200∠45°)
r1,1,2,4
r2,2,0,8j
m,r2,r3,1j
r3,0,o,5j
r4,o,0,10

analysis: ac
note: Determine the voltage Vo — aa(vo) reads 20.00 at -134.43°. Again the dotted node of each coupled element comes first.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/as7pp1301.png
omega: omega
rounding: 4
si: no
units: yes
rms: no
polar: yes


[AS7's Example 13.2 (mesh currents)]

e,1,0,100
r1,1,2,4-3j
r2,2,0,6j
m,r2,r3,2j
r3,3,2,8j
r4,3,0,5

analysis: ac
note: Calculate the mesh currents — aa(ir1) and aa(ir4). The node order matches the dots once more.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/as7e1302.png
omega: omega
rounding: 4
si: no
units: yes
